// ============================================================================
// Forgepoint Notification Service Proto Definitions
// ============================================================================
//
// WHY: The Notification service is the platform's "town crier" — it turns
// internal platform events (a pipeline failed, a model drifted, a quota was
// exceeded) into human-facing alerts (webhook, Slack, email). Crucially, it
// does this WITHOUT any other service knowing it exists.
//
// PATTERN — CHOREOGRAPHY (Pure Event Reactor):
//   In a CHOREOGRAPHY architecture there is NO central coordinator telling
//   services what to do next. Each service reacts to events it observes and
//   independently decides how to respond. Contrast with ORCHESTRATION (see the
//   Pipeline Orchestrator / saga), where one component issues explicit commands
//   ("now run step 2, now compensate step 1") and owns the workflow state.
//
//   The Notification service is the purest expression of choreography in
//   Forgepoint:
//     - It SUBSCRIBES to the entire event firehose (`fp.>` on NATS JetStream).
//     - For each event it matches the event's `type` against user-configured
//       RULES (e.g. rule pattern `fp.pipelines.*` matches both
//       `fp.pipelines.started` and `fp.pipelines.failed`).
//     - For each matching rule it DELIVERS the event over the rule's channel.
//   No producer ever calls this service. No producer knows a notification was
//   sent. Adding a brand-new event type requires ZERO code changes here — a
//   user just authors a rule whose pattern matches the new subject. THAT is the
//   payoff of choreography: extreme decoupling and independent evolvability.
//
//   THE OTHER SIDE OF THE COIN (interview tradeoff): choreography's weakness is
//   that no single place describes "the whole workflow". To answer "what
//   happens when a pipeline fails?" you must inspect every subscriber. We accept
//   that here because notification is a leaf reaction, not a multi-step business
//   transaction — there is no distributed state to coordinate or compensate.
//
// WHY THE gRPC SURFACE IS DELIBERATELY TINY:
//   The service's REAL work happens on the async (NATS) path — consuming events
//   and delivering them. Almost nothing about that flow is a synchronous
//   request/response, so it does not belong in gRPC. The gRPC API exists ONLY
//   for the human-facing CONTROL PLANE:
//     - reading the notifications I've been sent (inbox) — List/Get/MarkRead
//     - auditing whether my alerts actually went out — ListDeliveryAttempts
//     - configuring HOW and WHEN I want to be notified — Get/UpdatePreferences
//     - verifying a configured channel works — TestChannel (owner self-test)
//   That is the entire RPC surface. We resisted adding "SendNotification" or
//   "TriggerAlert" RPCs precisely because a PRODUCER calling us would VIOLATE
//   choreography — it would make us an orchestrated dependency. Producers emit
//   events; they never address us directly. (TestChannel is NOT such an RPC: it
//   is the OWNER asking us to test THEIR OWN channel, addressing no third party —
//   see its message doc for why that distinction matters.)
//
//   NOTE ON RULES: the implementation plan also mentions CreateRule/ListRules
//   for the matching engine. In this v1 contract we fold a user's routing
//   choices into NotificationPreferences (per-channel, per-event-category
//   toggles) rather than a free-form rule CRUD API, because that is the shape an
//   end user actually wants ("email me on failures, Slack me on drift"). A
//   richer admin rule-builder can arrive as a v2 addition without breaking this
//   surface. This tradeoff is called out so it is defensible in an interview.
//   The plan's third RPC, GetDeliveryLog, IS honored here as ListDeliveryAttempts
//   (the cross-notification delivery-health/audit query).
//
// REAL-WORLD COMPARISON:
//   - PagerDuty / Opsgenie: event intake → routing rules → notification
//     channels. Their "event rules" are exactly our pattern-matched rules.
//   - GitHub notifications: events (push, PR, mention) → per-user preferences →
//     web inbox + email. Our List/MarkRead inbox mirrors GitHub's notifications
//     center; UpdatePreferences mirrors GitHub's notification settings.
//   - AWS EventBridge → SNS: rules match event patterns, fan out to targets.
//   - Kafka consumer groups reacting to a topic with no producer awareness.
//
// DATA STORE: PostgreSQL (notifications inbox + delivery log + preferences).
//
// VERSIONING: Package path includes v1 following Buf/Google convention.
// Breaking changes require a new forgepoint.notification.v2 package.
// ============================================================================

